    <string name="settings_renderer_output_group_none">No group chosen. Tap to choose a server and a group.</string>
    <string name="settings_renderer_output_group_server">Group on server %1$s. Tap to change.</string>
    <string name="settings_renderer_output_group_named">%1$s on server %2$s. Tap to change.</string>
    <string name="settings_renderer_output_group_damaged">The stored group is damaged; the receiver plays on this device. Tap to choose the group again.</string>
    <string name="settings_renderer_output" translatable="false">settings_renderer_output</string>
    <string name="settings_renderer_output_group" translatable="false">settings_renderer_output_group</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_category">FCast</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_about_title">About FCast</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_about_summary">YAACC can play to FCast receivers on your network. This application is an independent community implementation of the FCast protocol. It is not affiliated with FUTO. FCast is a registered trademark of FUTO.</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_discovery" translatable="false">settings_fcast_discovery</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_discovery_title">Find FCast receivers</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_discovery_summary">Searches the local network for FCast receivers; on by default. It sends mDNS queries and lists every device that announces itself. Announcements are not authenticated: anyone on the network can announce a receiver. Nothing is played to a receiver before you choose it. Data is sent unencrypted with protocol version 3 and encrypted with version 4 (see below).</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_receivers" translatable="false">settings_fcast_receivers</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_receivers_title">FCast receivers</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_receivers_summary">Add a receiver by address, device name or connection link, or remove it</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_v4" translatable="false">settings_fcast_v4</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_v4_title">Use the encrypted version 4</string>
    <string name="settings_fcast_v4_summary">On Android 10 or later, a receiver whose key is known (from its connection link or its announcement) is spoken to in the encrypted FCast version 4. A key that does not match is refused, never answered with the unencrypted version. Turn this off to always use the unencrypted version 3, for example if a receiver does not work with version 4. A receiver that was added only by its address is always spoken to in version 3.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_category">Sendspin</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_about_title">About Sendspin</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_about_summary">YAACC can play music to Sendspin players on your network (experimental: players are found on the network or added by address). A player that only accepts paired servers is paired with the pairing token that the player shows. This application implements the Sendspin protocol except pairing with a code, and is not affiliated with the Open Home Foundation. Sendspin is a trademark of the Open Home Foundation.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_enabled" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_enabled</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_enabled_title">Use Sendspin players</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_enabled_summary">Off by default. When on, the players you added and the ones found on the network are listed as receivers and YAACC connects to them (outgoing connections only; nothing listens on this device). A player that you have not approved gets no audio; playing to it approves it.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_players" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_players</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_players_title">Sendspin players</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_players_summary">Add a player by address or remove it</string>
    <string name="sendspin_players_hint">Address, for example 192.168.0.7 or kitchen.local:8928</string>
    <string name="sendspin_players_add">Add</string>
    <string name="sendspin_players_remove">Remove %1$s</string>
    <string name="sendspin_players_none">No players added yet</string>
    <string name="sendspin_players_bad_address">That is not an address. Use a host name or IP address, with an optional port.</string>
    <string name="sendspin_players_too_many">At most 16 players can be added.</string>
    <string name="sendspin_players_already">This player is already in the list.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_discovery" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_discovery</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_discovery_title">Find Sendspin players</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_discovery_summary">Searches the local network for Sendspin players (mDNS) while a page with the receivers is open; on by default, but only while Sendspin players are switched on. It sends mDNS queries and lists every player that announces itself. Announcements are not authenticated: anyone on the network can announce a player. Nothing is played to a player before you choose it and play to it.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_controller_enabled" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_controller_enabled</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_controller_enabled_title">Control Sendspin servers</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_controller_enabled_summary">Off by default. When on, the servers you added (for example Music Assistant) appear among the receivers as cards that show what plays and control it: play, pause, stop, volume, next and more. Nothing is played to a server. Outgoing connections only.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_unpaired_servers" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_unpaired_servers</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_unpaired_servers_title">Allow unpaired servers</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_unpaired_servers_summary">On by default. A server you added may then show and control without pairing. The connection is encrypted but does not prove who the server is (the protocol says so); pairing as a client is not supported yet. Turn off to refuse such servers.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_servers" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_servers</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_servers_title">Sendspin servers</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_servers_summary">Add a server by address or remove it</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_server_discovery" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_server_discovery</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_server_discovery_title">Find Sendspin servers</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_server_discovery_summary">Searches the local network for Sendspin servers (mDNS) while a page with the receivers is open; on by default, but only while Control Sendspin servers is on. Found servers are not trusted: a server is connected to when its card is listed, and what it may do is up to its operator. Only addresses of the local network are accepted.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_legacy" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_legacy</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_legacy_title">Allow players without encryption (less secure)</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_legacy_summary">Off by default. Some older players, for example the Kodi add-on, speak an older version of the protocol without encryption, which the current specification does not define. When on, YAACC accepts them. Nothing they send or receive is protected: anyone on your network can read it and can pose as the player or as your phone, and the connection proves nothing about who the other side is. A player you have not approved still gets no audio. Use it only on a network you trust.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_advertise" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_advertise</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_advertise_title">Let players find this phone (listen on the network)</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_advertise_summary">Off by default. When on, and while a page with the receivers is open, this phone announces itself on the local network as a Sendspin server (mDNS, port 8927) and accepts connections from players that look for a server, for example the Kodi add-on. A player that is connected stays connected until its connection ends. Only addresses of the local network are accepted, at most 8 players at once, and a player you have not approved gets no audio. Nothing listens and nothing is announced while this is off.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_player" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_player</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_player_title">Let servers play on this phone</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_player_summary">Off by default. When on, the phone is a Sendspin player: a service with a notification keeps a listening socket open (port 8928) and announces the phone on the local network, so that Music Assistant or another server can play music on it. One server plays at a time (the rules of the protocol for several servers). Only addresses of the local network are accepted, at most 3 servers at once. Servers you paired may play; for others see Allow unpaired servers to play on this phone (off by default). The phone listens and announces itself as long as this is on.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_player_flac" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_player_flac</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_player_flac_title">Prefer FLAC for the phone as a player</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_player_flac_summary">Off by default. A server then sends FLAC (a smaller stream, about half of PCM) when it can, which the phone decodes with its own decoder; the same decoder path as the Snapcast client, not yet heard with a Sendspin server. PCM is still offered as the second choice. Takes effect for the next connection of a server.</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_player_unpaired" translatable="false">settings_sendspin_player_unpaired</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_player_unpaired_title">Allow unpaired devices to use this phone</string>
    <string name="settings_sendspin_player_unpaired_summary">Off by default. One setting for everything other devices can do on this phone: play on it (when it is a player) and control what it plays. A device that is not paired may then do this without proving who it is: any device on your network that finds the phone can connect (the connection is encrypted, but not authenticated). With this off, only devices you paired may: use *Add device* in the list of paired devices (scan the QR code or enter the token of the other phone), or, for a player, enter its token in the card of this phone.</string>
</resources>
